WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:05.800
Long before Al Capone turned his eye to bootlegging
and well before Bugsy Siegl decided to take

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:10.660
his ill-gotten gains and cash in on the casino
boom in Vegas, there was another boss in town.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:15.000
This one was perhaps the most unassuming of
ladies, and within just a few years of setting

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.520
foot on American soil for the first time,
she had built up one of the largest organized

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:23.160
crime empires the still-new nation had ever
seen.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:26.470
When the papers talked about her — and they
talked about her a lot — they called her

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:28.300
"Mother" Mandelbaum.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:29.300
Why?

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:33.000
As she once said herself, "I am Ma because
I give them what a mother cannot sometimes

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:37.200
give — money and horses and diamonds."

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:41.490
And she took care of her children, a vast
network of criminals from all walks of life

00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:44.030
and with all kinds of specialties.

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:48.680
She guided them, trained them, and when it
was necessary, she protected them… as only

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:52.210
a mother could.

00:00:52.210 --> 00:01:01.810
TITLE: Little Germany

00:01:01.810 --> 00:01:06.020
Her story starts in the middle of the 19th
century when people were coming from across

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:09.499
Europe to find a new life in the land of opportunity
and promise.

00:01:09.499 --> 00:01:13.759
Some were looking for a fresh start, some
were trying to escape the dire conditions

00:01:13.759 --> 00:01:17.030
of their homeland, and others were just looking
for adventure.

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:22.520
What they found when they got to America was
often a far cry from what they had imagined.

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:27.639
Fredericka Mandelbaum and her husband, Wolf
Israel, were just two of the hundreds of thousands

00:01:27.639 --> 00:01:31.119
of immigrants who poured into New York City
in the mid-1800s.

00:01:31.119 --> 00:01:36.479
Sources vary as to the exact date, but suggest
they left Europe around 1848 and settled in

00:01:36.479 --> 00:01:40.950
an area of New York City's Lower East Side
known as Little Germany.

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:45.799
It wasn't a great place, but it was a place
where like-minded and undoubtedly adventurous

00:01:45.799 --> 00:01:50.520
individuals could find others who shared a
history, a culture, and struggles.

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:52.119
And there were a lot of struggles.

00:01:52.119 --> 00:01:57.000
The typical tenement apartment was a mere
325 square feet, and most were home to as

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.180
many as 15 people.

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:04.229
Life in America wasn't as much about living
as it was about survival, and in order to

00:02:04.229 --> 00:02:09.140
survive, Mandelbaum and her husband spent
their days — up to 14 hours at a time — as

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:10.390
street peddlers.

00:02:10.390 --> 00:02:12.590
This likely wasn't an entirely new way of
life for them.

00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:16.610
You see, Mandelbaum was from one of the handful
of Jewish families living in the German state

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:21.970
of Hesse-Kassel, and they weren't just a minority,
they also existed on the outskirts of more

00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:23.560
mainstream society.

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:28.579
Jewish families like hers were often assaulted
and extorted, forced to remain on the move

00:02:28.579 --> 00:02:32.569
for their own safety — especially if they
didn't pay the protection money thugs often

00:02:32.569 --> 00:02:35.489
demanded in exchange for simply leaving them
alone.

00:02:35.489 --> 00:02:39.359
It's likely her father made his living as
a merchant and a peddler, buying and selling

00:02:39.359 --> 00:02:43.330
second-hand goods to support his family - but
this is just speculation.

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:46.830
For the early years of their marriage, she
and her husband lived the lives of wandering

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:47.840
peddlers as well.

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:55.909
It was a hard life, one where they could expect
to earn an average of around $6 per week.

00:02:55.909 --> 00:02:59.599
TITLE: From peddler to crime boss

00:02:59.599 --> 00:03:03.060
In August 1857, a massive hurricane sank a
ship called the Central America.

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:07.549
It had been carrying a huge amount of gold,
and without that gold, the economy also started

00:03:07.549 --> 00:03:08.549
to sink.

00:03:08.549 --> 00:03:13.319
It was a huge deal and caused a major financial
panic as businesses large and small started

00:03:13.319 --> 00:03:19.040
to fail, investments crashed, and people realised
they were suddenly facing unemployment and

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:20.040
ruin.

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:21.269
But something else happened, too.

00:03:21.269 --> 00:03:24.840
Desperate times call for desperate measures,
and with more and more people facing empty

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:28.299
kitchens and even emptier bellies, crime rose.

00:03:28.299 --> 00:03:29.299
A lot.

00:03:29.299 --> 00:03:33.159
A surge of children took to the streets, often
sent there by destitute parents desperate

00:03:33.159 --> 00:03:35.560
to find some way to feed their families.

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:40.209
Little hands were perfect for picking pockets
and stealing whatever wasn't nailed down,

00:03:40.209 --> 00:03:45.359
and even in the face of economic ruin, there
were a lot of opportunities for the most enterprising

00:03:45.359 --> 00:03:47.219
young scavengers.

00:03:47.219 --> 00:03:52.030
Children took to the dirty, littered streets
of the city to find anything that might help

00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:56.230
life become a little more bearable: a bit
of cotton that fell from a bale, flour and

00:03:56.230 --> 00:04:02.249
sugar carelessly spilled onto the street,
and just general junk.

00:04:02.249 --> 00:04:04.310
Sometimes these things were dropped and discarded.

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:06.700
Sometimes, they were stolen.

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:09.719
And all of those thieves needed a place to
sell their stolen goods.

00:04:09.719 --> 00:04:15.950
You can't eat jewelry, after all, and Mother
Mandelbaum saw a brilliant business opportunity.

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:19.769
Forging ahead without any real help from her
husband (who police would later describe as

00:04:19.769 --> 00:04:24.259
a "nonentity"), Mandelbaum started buying
and selling what those scavengers found, and

00:04:24.259 --> 00:04:28.930
she also started buying from the female pickpockets
and thieves that made their living nicking

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:33.669
stuff from some of the high-end department
stores that still catered to the city's wealthy.

00:04:33.669 --> 00:04:37.740
Mother Mandelbaum had a few things going for
her, and one of those things was her experience

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:41.270
not just buying and selling, but haggling
on prices.

00:04:41.270 --> 00:04:45.360
When she bought, she offered about a fifth
of what she could sell the item for — and

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:49.610
given that peddlers averaged only a few dollars
a week at the time selling the standard sort

00:04:49.610 --> 00:04:55.270
of fare, the markup that came with branching
out into stolen goods gave her a serious boost

00:04:55.270 --> 00:04:56.270
in income.

00:04:56.270 --> 00:04:57.460
And she made the most of it.

00:04:57.460 --> 00:05:01.050
Eight years after the crash, Mandelbaum had
built up the cash — and connections — to

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:04.240
be able to lease a building that contained
a dry goods store.

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:09.889
Rent was steep, and adjusted for inflation,
she paid an estimated $6100 and $6500 for

00:05:09.889 --> 00:05:11.710
her first and second year there.

00:05:11.710 --> 00:05:17.150
That's way more than her peddler's salary
would have realistically supported, so clearly,

00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:18.740
there was something fishy going on.

00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:24.500
But this was 19th century New York, and looking
the other way was a way of life.

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:28.889
The store was, indeed, just the front for
her operation, and it wasn't long before she

00:05:28.889 --> 00:05:34.470
was dealing in everything from jewels — particularly
diamonds — to fine silk and lace, silver,

00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:37.050
gold, and even horses and carriages.

00:05:37.050 --> 00:05:42.949
Once, she even bought and resold an entire
herd of goats, because if it made money, she

00:05:42.949 --> 00:05:43.949
was in.

00:05:43.949 --> 00:05:47.940
She was excellent at hiding what she was doing,
too, employing other German immigrants and

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:51.599
listing them as "salespeople" on census records.

00:05:51.599 --> 00:05:54.250
Technically, that was true.

00:05:54.250 --> 00:05:58.069
It wasn't just merchandise lifted off the
streets of New York that found its way into

00:05:58.069 --> 00:05:59.069
her warehouses, either.

00:05:59.069 --> 00:06:03.430
When the Great Chicago Fire swept through
the city in 1871, there was — unsurprisingly

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:06.520
— a ton of looting and pillaging going on
there.

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:10.720
A lot of what was stolen ended up passing
through her hands and her warehouses, and

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:11.720
it was all adding to her bottom line.

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:18.050
TITLE: A little bribery goes a long way

00:06:18.050 --> 00:06:23.120
If Mother Mandelbaum had simply been a fence,
just someone who only bought and sold stolen

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:27.020
goods for a considerable profit, the story
might have ended there.

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:31.550
But she wasn't just buying and selling stolen
goods, she built up a massive network of connections

00:06:31.550 --> 00:06:35.009
that stretched across the country and well
up into Canada.

00:06:35.009 --> 00:06:39.360
Even as she took more and more little pickpockets
under her wing, she would also turn around

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:43.190
and greet cops and judges — particularly
the ones that had a loose interpretation of

00:06:43.190 --> 00:06:45.320
the law — with a friendly smile.

00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:49.710
Even local politicians knew how popular she
was, and they were sure to keep on her good

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:50.710
side.

00:06:50.710 --> 00:06:52.759
There was some serious bribery going on there.

00:06:52.759 --> 00:06:57.500
And let's be completely honest: at the time,
New York City was filled with just as much

00:06:57.500 --> 00:07:00.000
corruption as cockroaches.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:04.580
It was a time when the city was under the
watchful eye of the notoriously crooked politicians

00:07:04.580 --> 00:07:10.110
of Tammany Hall, the entity behind the 1850's
City Council nicknamed the Forty Thieves.

00:07:10.110 --> 00:07:14.900
By the time Mother Mandelbaum's operation
was in full swing, Boss Tweed was in charge.

00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:18.629
Given that he was known for working in an
office that he opened solely to have an official

00:07:18.629 --> 00:07:24.759
place to take payments, it's safe to say he
was fine with all kinds of unlawful shenanigans.

00:07:24.759 --> 00:07:28.510
Tammany Hall in particular was well aware
of her operation, and made it a point to keep

00:07:28.510 --> 00:07:30.760
on Mother Mandelbaum's good side.

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:35.050
She couldn't vote, that's true, but she was
rapidly becoming very influential — and

00:07:35.050 --> 00:07:37.569
she knew plenty of people who could vote.

00:07:37.569 --> 00:07:42.389
One hand washes the other, and Mandelbaum's
empire continued to grow, while Tweed was

00:07:42.389 --> 00:07:47.151
quite literally given the keys to the city's
treasury in 1870 and started expanding on

00:07:47.151 --> 00:07:49.050
his own criminal enterprises.

00:07:49.050 --> 00:07:52.800
Tweed is a figure that history remembers as
being one of the most corrupt politicians

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:54.720
to ever rise to power in the city.

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:59.789
A New York State Senator with a list of other
titles and positions a mile long, Tweed used

00:07:59.789 --> 00:08:04.410
his political clout to swindle and steal an
unthinkable amount of money from the city's

00:08:04.410 --> 00:08:10.789
coffers — a haul that would, in today's
money, amount to around $3.5 billion dollars

00:08:10.789 --> 00:08:12.530
in ill-gotten gains.

00:08:12.530 --> 00:08:16.360
His Tammany Hall was a powerful ally for Mother
Mandelbaum to have.

00:08:16.360 --> 00:08:20.060
Meanwhile, Mandelbaum started hiring more
than just a few hungry street urchins.

00:08:20.060 --> 00:08:23.889
There was only so far they were going to be
able to take her, after all, and acquiring

00:08:23.889 --> 00:08:25.460
the loot was only half the job.

00:08:25.460 --> 00:08:29.939
She had to get rid of it, too, and so she
started building a team of associates to launder

00:08:29.939 --> 00:08:31.129
her merchandise.

00:08:31.129 --> 00:08:35.580
Engravers who could remove identifying marks
from jewelry were taken into her confidence,

00:08:35.580 --> 00:08:40.030
cab drivers were kept on call in case someone
needed to make a fast escape, and partners

00:08:40.030 --> 00:08:44.540
at a major law firm — Howe &amp; Hummell — were
kept on retainer… just in case.

00:08:44.540 --> 00:08:48.360
She even had a special fund for her most trusted
associates, and called it perhaps the most

00:08:48.360 --> 00:08:52.270
honest name she could have used: the Bureau
for the Protection of Criminals.

00:08:52.270 --> 00:08:56.340
The face of the city was changing, and Mother
Mandelbaum was there to take advantage of

00:08:56.340 --> 00:08:59.150
it every step of the way.

00:08:59.150 --> 00:09:02.870
TITLE: Branching out

00:09:02.870 --> 00:09:08.450
Sure, Mother Mandelbaum increased her wealth
exponentially when she branched out into selling

00:09:08.450 --> 00:09:10.870
stolen goods, but she didn't stop there.

00:09:10.870 --> 00:09:14.870
She was an entrepreneur, and entrepreneurs
are always looking for the next big thing.

00:09:14.870 --> 00:09:18.800
For her, it was bankrolling other criminal
enterprises undertaken by some of the most

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:22.360
audacious criminals in her little black book
of names.

00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:27.800
You've got to spend money to make money, the
old saying goes, and that's even true of crime.

00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:31.410
The countless immigrants who moved to the
young New York City helped build it on the

00:09:31.410 --> 00:09:37.000
shoulders of talents brought from the Old
World, and German immigrants excelled at engraving.

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:41.590
From woodcuts and prints it was just a short
hop, skip, and a jump into counterfeiting,

00:09:41.590 --> 00:09:44.860
and that was just one more aspect of her organization.

00:09:44.860 --> 00:09:48.510
Even as her own finances continued to climb,
she made it clear that if someone had a great

00:09:48.510 --> 00:09:52.980
plan and even better skills, she was willing
to bankroll jobs that would turn a profit.

00:09:52.980 --> 00:09:56.340
She was connected to some of the biggest bank
robbers at the time, including people like

00:09:56.340 --> 00:10:01.060
Wesley Allen and Michael Kurtz, and she also
provided the startup funds for a job that

00:10:01.060 --> 00:10:04.080
would turn into one of the biggest robberies
of them all.

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:08.840
A known associate of George Leonidas Leslie,
she provided him with around $65,000 in today's

00:10:08.840 --> 00:10:14.240
money to get the people and tools he needed
to rob the Manhattan Savings Bank in 1878.

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:21.460
And rob it, he did, netting $2,747,700 from
the bank frequented by people with names like

00:10:21.460 --> 00:10:24.140
Rockefeller, Vanderbilt, and Carnegie.

00:10:24.140 --> 00:10:27.030
To put that in perspective, you'll have to
adjust that for inflation.

00:10:27.030 --> 00:10:32.700
Today, that one score would be worth around
$72 million… give or take.

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:38.030
TITLE: Mother Mandelbaum's School for Enterprising
Criminals

00:10:38.030 --> 00:10:43.230
There was a certain sort of symbiosis to the
whole setup: without Mother Mandelbaum, the

00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:46.930
city's thieves and pickpockets would have
nowhere to launder their stolen goods.

00:10:46.930 --> 00:10:51.420
And without her ring of thieves, Mandelbaum
wouldn't have had a steady flow of goods to

00:10:51.420 --> 00:10:53.160
buy, mark up, and flip.

00:10:53.160 --> 00:10:56.610
It's no wonder she wanted to take care of
them, and in order to make sure her thieves

00:10:56.610 --> 00:11:01.830
were the very best they could be, legend says
she opened Mandelbaum's Grand Street School

00:11:01.830 --> 00:11:03.700
in 1870.

00:11:03.700 --> 00:11:07.670
It's an unassuming name, perhaps, but anyone
who walked through the doors of the storefront

00:11:07.670 --> 00:11:11.550
on Clinton and Grand Street was walking into
a school designed to give the city's street

00:11:11.550 --> 00:11:16.260
children some valuable life lessons — the
sort that involved checking your morals at

00:11:16.260 --> 00:11:17.260
the door.

00:11:17.260 --> 00:11:20.190
And there were plenty of children who needed
to learn if they were going to survive, as

00:11:20.190 --> 00:11:25.180
the city was teeming with orphaned children
known to the more fortunate as "street rats".

00:11:25.180 --> 00:11:29.990
The school started with the basics, and kids
were taught criminal life skills like pickpocketing

00:11:29.990 --> 00:11:31.440
and the art of misdirection.

00:11:31.440 --> 00:11:35.760
While there's no official transcripts from
the school — which isn't really surprising,

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:36.760
it's not the sort of thing you'd really want
to leave a written record of — it's believed

00:11:36.760 --> 00:11:40.950
that Mandelbaum recruited some of the city's
finest adult thieves and conmen to teach the

00:11:40.950 --> 00:11:42.500
children criminal skills.

00:11:42.500 --> 00:11:47.210
Those that showed an innate talent were taught
other things: blackmail, the art of the con,

00:11:47.210 --> 00:11:49.060
burglary, and even safe-cracking.

00:11:49.060 --> 00:11:53.490
And Mandelbaum recruited the best of the best
to come work directly for her, including one

00:11:53.490 --> 00:11:56.990
young woman who would become known as the
Princess of Crime.

00:11:56.990 --> 00:12:01.570
Sophie Lyons was one of Mandelbaum's star
students, and after getting her start learning

00:12:01.570 --> 00:12:06.630
from her pickpocket parents then being taken
under Mother's wing, Lyons became notorious

00:12:06.630 --> 00:12:10.950
as a con artist and thief who ran some seriously
brazen scams.

00:12:10.950 --> 00:12:15.170
One of her favorites was reportedly using
her feminine wiles to lure men into a hotel

00:12:15.170 --> 00:12:18.070
room, sweet-talk them out of their clothes,
then blackmail them.

00:12:18.070 --> 00:12:19.070
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sophie_Lyons#/media/File:Sophie_Lyons_in_Professional_Criminals_of_America.png

00:12:19.070 --> 00:12:22.390
Lyons eventually gave up her life of crime,
and condemned Mandelbaum for taking advantage

00:12:22.390 --> 00:12:26.960
of the lonely, hungry, and desperate, setting
them on a path of crime they may never have

00:12:26.960 --> 00:12:28.140
gone down otherwise.

00:12:28.140 --> 00:12:31.760
That's certainly one way to look at it, but
not everyone was so critical.

00:12:31.760 --> 00:12:35.450
In fact, most people had quite the opposite
view of their matronly crime lord.

00:12:35.450 --> 00:12:38.580
And as for the school, that was fairly short-lived.

00:12:38.580 --> 00:12:42.500
Mandelbaum shut it down herself in 1876, after
only six years.

00:12:42.500 --> 00:12:44.040
Why shut down something so successful?

00:12:44.040 --> 00:12:48.040
That’s because she got wind of a new student
who had enrolled, and she was too smart to

00:12:48.040 --> 00:12:52.290
let the son of a police officer in on their
secrets.

00:12:52.290 --> 00:12:57.380
TITLE: Tea and crumpets, anyone?

00:12:57.380 --> 00:13:01.670
Mother Mandelbaum's massive criminal empire
spanned from the lowliest pickpocket to the

00:13:01.670 --> 00:13:04.630
upper echelons of New York City's political
landscape.

00:13:04.630 --> 00:13:08.320
No one can be at work all the time, though,
and when Mandelbaum wasn't dealing in stolen

00:13:08.320 --> 00:13:11.680
jewels or herds of goats, she was far from
frightening.

00:13:11.680 --> 00:13:15.190
She didn't prowl the streets with a gun, just
looking for trouble, she didn't rule her neighborhood

00:13:15.190 --> 00:13:19.420
with an iron fist, and in fact, by all accounts,
she was quite nice.

00:13:19.420 --> 00:13:23.700
When her obituary ran in the German-language
paper the New York Volkszeitung, it described

00:13:23.700 --> 00:13:29.110
her as "honorable and reliable", and as someone
whose "word was as good as gold".

00:13:29.110 --> 00:13:32.840
When New York City's law enforcement was asked
about her character, Police Chief George W.

00:13:32.840 --> 00:13:38.150
Wailing described her this way: "As a woman
and a mother, [she] is spoken of with respect."

00:13:38.150 --> 00:13:42.850
Others spoke of her as a "wonderful woman",
a "woman of ability and nerve," and lauded

00:13:42.850 --> 00:13:44.550
her for her generosity.

00:13:44.550 --> 00:13:48.020
It wasn't just her generosity people spoke
highly of, it was also, her parties.

00:13:48.020 --> 00:13:49.020
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fredericka_Mandelbaum#/media/File:Fredericka_Mendelbaum2.jpg

00:13:49.020 --> 00:13:51.680
At the same time she was buying and selling
and dealing, Mandelbaum was living in a house

00:13:51.680 --> 00:13:56.580
on the Lower East Side, where she held soirees
and sent out invitations from "The Honorable

00:13:56.580 --> 00:13:58.720
and Mrs. William Mandelbaum".

00:13:58.720 --> 00:14:03.550
The parties were sometimes elegant, formal
affairs, sometimes summertime picnics, but

00:14:03.550 --> 00:14:04.550
always exciting.

00:14:04.550 --> 00:14:08.610
The guest lists would include all of New York's
most prominent judges and politicians… along

00:14:08.610 --> 00:14:10.100
with some of her criminal empire.

00:14:10.100 --> 00:14:15.340
It would allow "business acquaintances" to
rub elbows, schmooze over cocktails and ultimately

00:14:15.340 --> 00:14:19.471
further their own movements upward through
the hierarchy of crime, and that was fine

00:14:19.471 --> 00:14:20.471
by her.

00:14:20.471 --> 00:14:23.840
At any rate, aside from the whole criminal
empire thing, Mother Mandelbaum was almost

00:14:23.840 --> 00:14:25.470
shockingly conventional.

00:14:25.470 --> 00:14:29.990
She was a generous and respected member of
her synagogue, she invited her "associates"

00:14:29.990 --> 00:14:33.730
into her home to visit with her children,
and when those in her organization needed

00:14:33.730 --> 00:14:35.090
help, she provided it.

00:14:35.090 --> 00:14:39.210
And that's likely one of the things that allowed
her to stay in business for so long.

00:14:39.210 --> 00:14:42.590
When it came to dealing with those in her
own community, she was known for offering

00:14:42.590 --> 00:14:46.920
jobs when they were needed, and even undercutting
the prices offered by legitimate wholesalers,

00:14:46.920 --> 00:14:49.930
and giving struggling families the help they
badly needed.

00:14:49.930 --> 00:14:53.740
When it came time for law enforcement to bring
her down, they knew they were on their own

00:14:53.740 --> 00:14:56.750
- her close-knit community would not betray
her.

00:14:56.750 --> 00:14:59.390
TITLE: One bad apple to spoil the bunch

00:14:59.390 --> 00:15:05.240
So, here's a question: where were the police
in all of this?

00:15:05.240 --> 00:15:08.350
Surely, bribery can only get you so far, and
it just takes one person who's not willing

00:15:08.350 --> 00:15:12.410
to take some cash and look the other way.

00:15:12.410 --> 00:15:16.070
New York City's police department was fairly
new at the time, and they hadn't quite figured

00:15:16.070 --> 00:15:18.320
out what their role was in the growing city.

00:15:18.320 --> 00:15:22.240
When they were on the streets, they were looking
for some specific things: gang-related brawls,

00:15:22.240 --> 00:15:26.870
drunk and disorderly disruptions, and the
vocal, troublesome crowds that tended to congregate

00:15:26.870 --> 00:15:31.160
when social reformers and the like got a little
too preachy on their soap-boxes.

00:15:31.160 --> 00:15:33.780
When it came to criminals like Mandelbaum,
she was parked firmly in the blind spot of

00:15:33.780 --> 00:15:38.030
a police department who would only negotiate
for the return of stolen goods if the victim

00:15:38.030 --> 00:15:39.590
paid them to do so.

00:15:39.590 --> 00:15:43.570
And this was an official policy, not just
some corrupt cops doing some shady stuff to

00:15:43.570 --> 00:15:45.200
see what they can fill their pockets with.

00:15:45.200 --> 00:15:48.790
Since it was sometimes easier for victims
to just forget about what they'd lost, thieves

00:15:48.790 --> 00:15:53.120
and fences dealing in stolen goods knew they
weren't a high priority.

00:15:53.120 --> 00:15:58.030
Still, it's wildly impressive that Mother
Mandelbaum managed to stay out of any real,

00:15:58.030 --> 00:16:00.600
serious trouble for around 30 years.

00:16:00.600 --> 00:16:04.620
So, what was it that finally brought down
her criminal empire?

00:16:04.620 --> 00:16:07.660
A bolt of silk and the Pinkerton Detective
Agency.

00:16:07.660 --> 00:16:12.210
It wasn't until 1884 that a New York District
Attorney named Peter Olson had finally had

00:16:12.210 --> 00:16:15.270
enough of the Queen of the Fences, and turned
to the Pinkertons for help.

00:16:15.270 --> 00:16:19.390
They sent Detective Gustave Frank, who got
an introduction through someone perhaps not

00:16:19.390 --> 00:16:21.940
as loyal as Mandelbaum had thought.

00:16:21.940 --> 00:16:25.690
When police finally raided her warehouse,
they found the silk that the undercover Pinkerton

00:16:25.690 --> 00:16:31.410
had sold her, and a ton of stolen merchandise.

00:16:31.410 --> 00:16:33.050
How much stolen loot?

00:16:33.050 --> 00:16:36.590
One contemporary journalist wrote, "It did
not seem possible that so much wealth could

00:16:36.590 --> 00:16:38.550
be assembled in one spot.

00:16:38.550 --> 00:16:41.030
There seemed to be enough clothes to supply
an army.

00:16:41.030 --> 00:16:44.280
There were trunks filled with precious gems
and silverware.

00:16:44.280 --> 00:16:46.930
Antique furniture was stacked against a wall."

00:16:46.930 --> 00:16:50.880
And keep in mind, this is at a time when children
were playing in the streets alongside the

00:16:50.880 --> 00:16:56.570
decomposing remains of carriage horses left
where they had fallen, and when many parents

00:16:56.570 --> 00:16:59.190
weren't sure how they were going to feed their
families.

00:16:59.190 --> 00:17:03.910
Most wouldn't have been able to envision holding
a single earring, much less be able to imagine

00:17:03.910 --> 00:17:06.899
a whole suitcase of precious jewels.

00:17:06.899 --> 00:17:10.910
There should have been enough there to put
her away for a long time, but remarkably,

00:17:10.910 --> 00:17:11.910
that didn't happen.

00:17:11.910 --> 00:17:16.740
Mandelbaum, her son, and her clerk insisted
that they were simply running a dry goods

00:17:16.740 --> 00:17:19.459
store… which technically was almost true.

00:17:19.459 --> 00:17:24.009
She claimed she had never stolen anything,
which again… you've got to give her that.

00:17:24.009 --> 00:17:27.459
In all her career, she never did steal anything
herself.

00:17:27.459 --> 00:17:31.940
Mandelbaum was let out on bail, which she
promptly jumped and headed north into Canada.

00:17:31.940 --> 00:17:33.710
And the story still isn't done.

00:17:33.710 --> 00:17:37.149
Ten years later, Mandelbaum had set herself
up as a respectable sort of citizen living

00:17:37.149 --> 00:17:42.010
in Hamilton, Ontario, working in her hat shop
and participating in the Anshe Sholem Hebrew

00:17:42.010 --> 00:17:43.010
Congregation.

00:17:43.010 --> 00:17:45.490
Was there more to it than that?

00:17:45.490 --> 00:17:46.490
Possibly.

00:17:46.490 --> 00:17:50.940
At any rate, by 1894 she had found herself
pining for her old stomping grounds in the

00:17:50.940 --> 00:17:52.169
13th Ward.

00:17:52.169 --> 00:17:55.490
She made that known, too, telling friends
and acquaintances just as much.

00:17:55.490 --> 00:17:58.230
Then, in February of that year, Mandelbaum
passed away.

00:17:58.230 --> 00:18:02.240
A coffin was headed from Ontario to New York
City… but here's the thing.

00:18:02.240 --> 00:18:07.009
No one really knew if it was actually her
body or just one more brilliant cog in the

00:18:07.009 --> 00:18:11.230
well-oiled machine that had allowed her to
stay a free woman for so long.

00:18:11.230 --> 00:18:15.120
Did she fake her death so she could return
to her beloved city in peace?

00:18:15.120 --> 00:18:16.120
People thought she might have.

00:18:16.120 --> 00:18:21.330
Was she now calling herself Madame Fuchs,
and returning to her roots as a simple peddler?

00:18:21.330 --> 00:18:22.539
It's entirely possible.

00:18:22.539 --> 00:18:26.989
No one ever really knew, just as no one knows
just how much money she handled in her lifetime.

00:18:26.989 --> 00:18:30.850
Estimates vary, with one suggesting that when
she fled New York in 1884, she left behind

00:18:30.850 --> 00:18:36.809
merchandise that, in today's money, would
be worth around $24 million.

00:18:36.809 --> 00:18:57.570
Give or take.

